# AI Execution Framework Rules

These rules apply to ALL agents working in this project. They are non-negotiable invariants.

## Source of Truth

- `ai-framework/LOGIC.md` is the authoritative workflow specification. Follow it exactly.
- `ai-framework/project-profile.md` defines language-specific conventions. Read it before any work.
- `ARCHITECTURE.md` at the project root defines the domain dependency policy. All agents must respect the domain dependency DAG. Circular cross-domain dependencies are strictly prohibited.
- Taskwarrior state is the source of truth for execution progress, not filesystem layout or git status.
- All Taskwarrior read-only commands must use `taskwarrior/tw`, never bare `task`. This ensures per-project database isolation.

## Absolute Script Paths (Isolation Invariant)

- Invoke every framework script as `bash <tree-root>/taskwarrior/<script>`: the main project root for PM scripts, the epic worktree root for Coordinator and phase scripts.
- Subagents cannot be given a working directory, so an agent always starts in the main project tree. A relative script path therefore targets the wrong tree, which is how a Coordinator corrupts main.
- Never `cd` into a worktree as a substitute. Each script sources `taskwarrior/guard.sh`, resolves its own tree, and exits 2 when invoked against the wrong one. Exit 2 from the guard means wrong path: fix the path, do not retry.
- `.taskrc` is generated per tree by `taskwarrior/setup.sh` and is gitignored. Never commit it; its UDAs are mode-specific and a worktree copy would corrupt main on merge.

## Subagent Model Selection

- Every agent's model is pinned in its own prompt frontmatter, assigned by bucket at deploy time via `ai-framework/set-agent-models.sh`.
- **Never pass a `model` parameter when invoking a subagent.** That argument overrides the frontmatter and silently replaces a deliberate capability assignment with the parent's model.
- Do not edit `model:` lines by hand. Re-run the script so a whole bucket stays consistent.

## Hierarchy

The framework uses a four-level hierarchy:

- **Project** (mandatory): `plan/project.md` pins a Paavo Notes closed version and an ordered milestone roadmap to product completion
- **Milestone**: derived from the roadmap; groups related epics; Status Done / In Progress / TODO
- **Epic**: the unit of parallel execution; gets its own git worktree; contains ordered stories
- **Story**: a vertical feature slice executed serially within its epic

Epics run in parallel (separate worktrees). Stories within an epic run serially (same worktree).

## Paavo Notes MCP (Hard Dependency)

- Product intent lives in Paavo Notes. The framework caches a pinned execution roadmap in `plan/project.md`.
- The Paavo Notes MCP is required for all framework work. If unreachable, the PM hard-stops and requirements agents escalate. Do not invent product goals.
- Always read a single **closed integer version** pinned in `plan/project.md`. Never query the open/live version for execution.
- Agents discover MCP tool names/signatures via Cursor MCP tool listing. Do not hardcode tool APIs.
- **Who may access Paavo Notes**: PM, Roadmap Planner, and the four requirements-phase agents only.
- **Who must not**: Coordinator, architecture / integration-test / implementation agents, story-review, escalation agents, fixer, and general agents.
- Open questions: PM and requirements-plan/write may **post** append-only open questions against the pinned version. Agents must never list, read, deduplicate, answer, or delete existing open questions. Non-blocking gaps: post and continue. Blocking gaps: escalate.
- Local discoveries are for code/impl findings; product-intent gaps belong in Paavo Notes.

## Script-Only State Mutations

Agents must NEVER call `taskwarrior/tw` directly for state changes (modify, add, done, start, stop, annotate, denotate, delete). All state mutations go through the provided scripts under `taskwarrior/`:

- PM uses: `pm-lock-acquire`, `pm-lock-release`, `epic-fork`, `epic-merge`, `epic-mark-ready`, `epic-rebase`, `epic-gate-release`
- Coordinator uses: `coordinator-lock-acquire`, `coordinator-lock-release`, `story-init`, `story-next`, `story-complete`, `story-merge`, `phase-start`, `phase-stop`, `phase-transition`, `phase-annotate`, `phase-done`, `phase-block`
- Phase agents use: `phase-annotate`, `phase-transition`
- Read-only queries (epic-status, pm-preflight, epic-gate-status, coordinator-lock-status, coordinator-status, `doctor` without `--fix`, `taskwarrior/tw ... export/count`) are allowed directly.
- `doctor --fix` is reserved for the `environment-recovery` agent and the user. No other agent may run it, and no agent may pass `--force`.
- `coordinator-heartbeat` is called by the lifecycle scripts. Agents never call it directly and never write telemetry by hand.

Scripts enforce preconditions, validate transitions, and exit non-zero on violations. Agents react to exit codes.

## State Discipline

- Agents must never advance more than one state transition per invocation (e.g. plan -> plan-review, not plan -> write).
- Every artifact written must be annotated on the corresponding Taskwarrior task via `phase-annotate`.
- The Coordinator is the only agent that creates Taskwarrior tasks (via `story-init`), marks tasks done (via `phase-done`), or manages git branches.
- At most one Taskwarrior task may be `+ACTIVE -AI_LOCK` at any time per worktree. The Coordinator verifies this via `phase-start` (which exits 1 if another task is active).
- Only one PM may run at a time (global lock in main tree). Use `pm-lock-acquire`.
- Only one Coordinator may run per epic worktree. Use `coordinator-lock-acquire`.
- Duplicate PM/Coordinator agents must not modify state. They report status and exit.
- Stale locks and orphaned active tasks must only be cleared by the user after confirming no agents are running, via `bash taskwarrior/cleanup-ai-state.sh --apply`.

## Merge Gate

- Only one epic may merge to main at a time. The merge gate is enforced by `epic-merge`.
- No new epic worktrees may be created while a merge is in progress (`epic-fork` checks the gate).
- The PM never directly manipulates the gate. Only `epic-merge` and `epic-fork` scripts interact with it.
- Stale gate recovery: `bash taskwarrior/epic-gate-release --force` (manual only, after confirming no merge is in progress).

## Review Standards

- Review agents must cite specific files and issues. "Looks good" without evidence is forbidden.
- Review agents must not nitpick formatting or style. Focus on correctness, completeness, and structural integrity.
- The Coordinator enforces the 3-rejection limit per phase and writes an escalation on the 3rd rejection.

## Implementation Standards

- Implementation agents must never: hardcode test values, write empty stubs, use mocks in production code, skip error handling, or silently deviate from the architecture.
- If implementation is impossible given the architecture, escalate. Do not silently work around it.

## Context Discipline

- The PM never reads source code, tests, or plan artifacts below the story level, except `plan/discoveries/` during Discovery Triage.
- The Coordinator never reads code, requirements, architecture, tests, or review content. It only reads Taskwarrior output from scripts.
- Phase agents read only what their role specifies.

## Escalation

- If an agent cannot complete its task, it must write an escalation file to `plan/escalations/`, annotate the task via `bash taskwarrior/phase-annotate`, and **exit immediately**.
- Escalation files use the template from `plan/templates/escalation.md`.
- Escalations halt Coordinator work. The Coordinator blocks the task via `phase-block`, releases its lock, and returns control to the PM.
- The PM classifies every escalation with `escalation-triage` (read-only) before any recovery, and only after verifying the Coordinator lock is free in the worktree.
- Routing is mechanical: `environment` -> `environment-recovery`, `artifact` -> `escalation-recovery`, `product-intent` and `scope-policy` -> the user. Any low-confidence result goes to the user.
- One automatic attempt per root-cause fingerprint. If the fingerprint already appears in the task's annotations, route to the user instead of retrying.
- Environment damage is mechanical and must not be dumped on the user; product and scope decisions must never be automated away.

## Coordinator Supervision

- Coordinators run in the background so epics execute in parallel.
- The PM supervises exclusively through `bash taskwarrior/coordinator-status` and acts on its exit code: 0 healthy, 1 stale (re-check once), 2 dead / never started / escalated.
- Liveness comes from a heartbeat the lifecycle scripts write automatically, so silence means stuck work.
- NEVER infer Coordinator progress by reading agent transcripts, chat logs, or `.jsonl` files.

## Discoveries

- Any agent may write one new discovery file under `plan/discoveries/` using `plan/templates/discovery.md`, then continue its task.
- Discovery filenames: `YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-slug.md`.
- Subagents must NEVER read, list, search, deduplicate, modify, or delete existing discovery files.
- Only the PM triages discoveries after milestone completion.

## Git

- One worktree per epic, branched from main (`epic/EXXXX-slug`).
- One branch per story within the worktree (`story/XXXXX-slug`).
- Commit after phase review approval only (except PM committing planning artifacts to main).
- Never force-push or rebase shared branches.

## Entry Point Enforcement

This framework only works when the `project-manager` agent drives the pipeline. If you are NOT the `project-manager`, `coordinator`, `fixer`, `escalation-triage`, `escalation-recovery`, `environment-recovery`, `roadmap-planner`, or a named phase agent, you are a **general agent**:

- You must NOT write code, requirements, architecture artifacts, tests, or framework artifacts.
- You must NOT invoke the Coordinator or phase agents yourself.
- If the user asks to implement a feature or "run the Coordinator," tell them to open a new chat with the **`project-manager`** agent.

The `roadmap-planner` agent may write `plan/project.md` only when the PM invokes it.

Only designated phase-write agents may create or modify files in their respective directories.

The `escalation-recovery` agent may modify artifacts only for bounded corrections to the current story, and only when the PM invokes it after a clean Coordinator halt.

The `escalation-triage` agent writes nothing at all. The `environment-recovery` agent may run only its whitelisted commands (`doctor`, `doctor --fix`, `coordinator-status`, `epic-status`, read-only `tw`, `setup.sh --worktree`) and may write only an appended `## Recovery Result` section in the escalation file. It may never run a `git` command or mutate Taskwarrior directly.

The `fixer` agent may modify source and test files for bug fixes only. It does not use Taskwarrior and operates outside the PM pipeline.

## Forbidden

- Read and respect the "Forbidden" section in `ai-framework/project-profile.md`.
